# Print files are bytes, not text. Git must not touch their line endings.
#
# Why (2026-09-19): with the usual Windows `core.autocrlf=true` and no rule here,
# the demo job's G-code is stored LF in the repo and checked out CRLF -- one
# extra byte on every one of its 124,722 lines, 3,088,226 bytes in the working
# tree against 2,963,504 in the commit. Everything still parses, so it looks
# harmless, until something compares a fresh slice with the committed file: the
# slicer writes LF and the comparison fails on a machine whose git rewrote the
# reference. That is exactly what happened to test/server-slice.js, and the
# byte-identical re-slice -- the claim the whole re-slice lane rests on -- looked
# broken when it was not.
#
# A checkout of a print file must be the file that was committed.
*.gcode -text
*.stl   -text
*.3mf   -text
